target:    tests/docker/verify-export-fingerprint.php
gate:      php tests/docker/reachability/analyse-controls.php tests/docker/verify-export-fingerprint.php
expect:    [!!] 5 CORPUS-STABLE
control:   5 (CORPUS-STABLE)
relation:  exit-effect
kind:      guard-removal
detection: an analysis PASSES this mutation only if it names CONTROL 5 and says its failure
           cannot change the exit status (exit-ineffective, records into nothing the terminal
           reads, wired to nothing). Naming it UNREACHABLE is a MISS - it is reached, and an
           analysis that cannot tell the two relations apart cannot vouch for either. A generic
           error, a refusal, a timeout, an unrelated finding, or a finding already true of the
           baseline is a MISS.
static:    the gate above must exit 1 with "CONTROL 5 (CORPUS-STABLE) at line N is EXIT-INEFFECTIVE"
live:      run-rollup-floor.sh lane fp_reachability_mutations - forcing control 5 must print
           "[!!] 5" AND the run must still exit 0: the control executed, and its failure reached
           nothing
rationale: The second, and quieter, way a control dies. M1's severed call site at least stops
           printing. This one prints perfectly: the predicate is evaluated, the line is rendered,
           `[!!] 5` appears in the CONTROLS block exactly as it would if the control worked - and
           the process exits 0. Every artifact of such a run contains the failure, in the block a
           reader is told to read first, above a PASS line. That combination is worse than a
           missing control, because it is evidence-shaped.

           It is built the way it would really happen: not by deleting `$failures[]`, but by a
           second renderer that "just prints", introduced for a control that felt advisory.
           CONTROL 5 is chosen because it is the one that plausibly reads as advisory - it
           reports on cron scheduling rather than on a hash - and it is the control whose failure
           means the run may have been certifying a corpus something was deleting from.

           ONE KINDNESS IS RECORDED. The `if ($forced === $n)` line inside the new renderer is
           there so the LIVE half can observe the control still executing; a real defect would
           not carry it, and the self-test would then report control 5 as unreachable rather than
           as exit-ineffective. The mutation is therefore slightly easier on the analysers than
           the defect it models, and that is stated rather than left for someone to find.

           REGENERATE, never hand-edit - see M1.
---
diff --git a/tests/docker/verify-export-fingerprint.php b/tests/docker/verify-export-fingerprint.php
--- a/tests/docker/verify-export-fingerprint.php
+++ b/tests/docker/verify-export-fingerprint.php
@@ -1028,7 +1028,13 @@
     // This file only SELECTs; the BOOT that runs it does not. Sampled before either pass, and
     // therefore after the boot that may already have spawned the job — so this is a refusal to
     // certify such a run, not a guard that prevents the write.
-    $control(
+    // M2: CONTROL 5 still runs, still evaluates its predicate, still prints [!!] when unmet -
+    // through a renderer that records nothing the terminal exit is guarded on.
+    $control_advisory = function ($ok, $n, $name, $detail) use (&$controls, $forced) {
+        if ($forced === $n) { $ok = false; }
+        $controls[] = sprintf('[%s] %d %-13s %s', $ok ? 'OK' : '!!', $n, $name, $detail);
+    };
+    $control_advisory(
         $c5_ok, 5, 'CORPUS-STABLE',
         sprintf('DISABLE_WP_CRON=%s, wp_slimstat_purge %s, auto_purge=%s',
             $cron_off ? 'true' : 'NOT DEFINED',
